---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Calculation conventions"
  description="Understand fixed durations, calendar dates, time zones, rounding, and browser timer limits."
  path="/conventions/"
>
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Calculation guide</p>
    <h1>Know what your time means.</h1>
    <h2>Durations and clock readings</h2>
    <p>
      Enter durations as H:MM or H:MM:SS. Minutes and seconds stay below 60, and
      hours may exceed 24. Decimal arithmetic avoids binary rounding during
      intermediate work. Durations display milliseconds, decimal amounts display
      up to six places, and frame durations display up to six fractional
      seconds.
    </p>
    <p>
      Clock-reading tools use fixed arithmetic. A day is 24 hours and a week is
      7 days in the unit converter. These values do not describe every real
      calendar day when daylight saving changes.
    </p>
    <h2>Calendar dates</h2>
    <p>
      Dates use the ISO Gregorian calendar. Date differences exclude the end
      date. Business-day calculations let you choose whether to include it.
      Calendar month arithmetic clamps to the last valid day when the original
      day is unavailable.
    </p>
    <h2>Time zones</h2>
    <p>
      City zones follow the rules in your browser’s time-zone database. Fixed
      abbreviations such as PST (UTC−08:00) do not observe daylight saving.
      Missing local times are rejected. Repeated times require a choice between
      earlier and later occurrences.
    </p>
    <h2>Timers and sharing</h2>
    <p>
      Running duration timers use a finishing timestamp rather than counting
      each browser tick. Pause stores the remaining duration; resume sets a new
      finishing timestamp. Deadlines preserve an absolute instant and cannot
      pause. Your device clock matters.
    </p>
    <p>
      A shared duration reopens a preset. A shared deadline retains its absolute
      instant across devices. Links never start a timer automatically. Session
      storage restores active timing within the same tab, while favourites and
      display settings stay in local storage.
    </p>
    <h2>Estimates</h2>
    <p>
      Speech uses the entered words-per-minute rate plus extra pauses. Render
      estimates assume equal workers and whole frame batches, plus your overhead
      percentage. Frame conversion uses an explicit frame rate; it does not
      implement SMPTE or drop-frame timecode.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
